Actually, there's a subtle bug: the tampered token's signature differs by one base64 char. But base64url decoding: the signature is 32 bytes. Changing last char 'Y'→'Z': base64 chars. 'Y' = 24, 'Z' = 25. As the last base64 character of a 43-char string (no padding), the last char contributes only 4 significant bits (since 32 bytes = 256 bits = 43 base64 chars * 6 = 258 bits, last char has 2 unused bits). So changing the last char from 'Y' (011000) to 'Z' (011001) changes only the LAST 2 BITS which are discarded during decoding! So both decode to the SAME 32 bytes! Therefore the tampered token has the SAME signature bytes and verification passes.

This is a real defect: the "被篡改签名" sample is not actually tampered — changing the final base64url character of a 32-byte HMAC doesn't change the decoded bytes. So the demo of "签名不通过" fails; a user clicking it sees "签名验证通过", which is misleading and contradicts the design variant and the tool's own text ("验签必然失败").

